  "narrative": "## Target shape  \nThe migration will produce two extraction waves. Wave 0 contains the five tightly-coupled subsystems that currently form a dependency cycle; after cutting the three cheapest links they can be extracted in the order #4 \u2192 #1 + #3 \u2192 #0 + #2. Wave 1 holds the remaining subsystem, which depends only on wave 0 components. All interfaces will be introduced at the entry points already listed in the facts.\n\n## Migration sequence  \n- **Wave 0** \u2013 cut the three cheapest cycle links and then extract:  \n  1. **#4** \u2013 expose its `registerHints` entry point as a fa\u00e7ade over the runtime-hint registration logic; the only blocker is the need to cut the link from #4 to #2.  \n  2. **#1** and **#3** \u2013 each gets a fa\u00e7ade over its public entry points (`processFindForm` for #1 and the `Pet` class API for #3). Both are blocked by the link from #1 to #0, which must be cut before they can be extracted.  \n  3. **#0** and **#2** \u2013 each receives a fa\u00e7ade over its primary entry points (`processUpdateForm` for #0 and `addPaginationModel` for #2). Their blockers are the remaining cut links (#3 \u2192 #0 and #4 \u2192 #2).  \n\n- **Wave 1** \u2013 **#6** can be extracted after wave 0 is complete; it will expose its `processNewVisitForm` method behind a fa\u00e7ade. The only blocker is its weak boundary (no internal dependencies), so no additional cut is required.\n\n## Biggest risks  \n- Cutting the link #3 \u2192 #0 may break existing callers that rely on the direct `Pet`-to-`PetController` interaction.  \n- Removing the link #4 \u2192 #2 could affect the runtime-hint registration that the Vet subsystem expects.  \n- The link #1 \u2192 #0 is the most expensive to cut; failing to replace it cleanly may cause Owner-related requests to break.  \n- Low cohesion in several subsystems (especially #0, #1, #3, #4) means the extracted modules may have large public surfaces, increasing maintenance overhead.  \n- Instability scores above 0.8 for #0, #2, and #6 indicate they are likely to change frequently, which could cause churn in the newly created interfaces.\n\n## What this analysis cannot tell you  \n- The functional impact of the introduced fa\u00e7ades on runtime behavior or performance.  \n- Whether the cut links can be replaced with simple delegations or require more extensive refactoring.  \n- The exact effort needed to rewrite tests that currently cross the cycle boundaries.  \n- Any hidden runtime configuration (e.g., Spring bean wiring) that may depend on the current package structure.",
